# Changelog

Apply an inline-specified dynamic search rule to a search query.

Search query objects in the following endpoints now have an additional, optional `inlineRule` parameter.

When present and not `null`, then Meilisearch will check if query satisfies the conditions of the dynamic search rule specified in `inlineRule`, and if so will apply the actions of the rule as if it had been sent to the `PATCH /dynamicSearchRule/{:uid}` endpoint.

Additionally, if the `Meili-Include-Metadata` header is present in the request with the value `true`, then the `metadata` in the response will contain information about whether the conditions of the inline rule were satisfied and if not why.

Meilisearch Cloud will use this backend feature to implement a preview UI for dynamic search rules.

Using `inlineRule` requires Meilisearch Enterprise Edition and the `dynamicSearchRules` experimental feature.

## Details

1. There is a new `InlineRule` object with the following field:
    1. `uid`, string, mandatory: unique ID of the rule. If there is already a rule with this id, then the inline rule will replace the existing rule for the duration of this search query.
    2. `active`, boolean, optional, defaults to `true`: whether the rule is active. The actions of an inactive inline rule never apply to the search, but its conditions are still checked
    3. `conditions`: `RuleConditions` object, identical to the `conditions` field in the body of `PATCH /dynamic-search-rules/{:uid}`
    4. `actions`: `RuleActions` object, identical to the `actions` field in the body of `PATCH /dynamic-search-rules/{:uid}`
    5. `precedence`: integer, optional, identical to the `precedence` field in the body of `PATCH /dynamic-search-rules/{:uid}`
2. The `POST /indexes/{:indexUid}/search` body has an additional, optional (defaults to `null`) `inlineRule` field which is an `InlineRule` object.
3. In the `POST /multi-search` body, the objects inside the `queries` array have an additional, optional (defaults to `null`) `inlineRule` field which is an `InlineRule` object.

## Examples

<details>

<summary>Passing a search rule specific to the user making the request</summary>

```jsonc
// POST /indexes/movies/search
{
  "q": "super hero",
  "inlineRule": {
    "uid": "user-likes-batman",
    "actions": {
      "scale": [
        {
          // filter recent Batman movies
          "filter": "hero = batman AND release_time = fresh",
          "weight": 1.2 // moderate relevancy boost
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

</details>

<details>

<summary>Previewing the effect of a search rule</summary>

```jsonc
// POST /multi-search -H 'Meili-Include-Metadata: true`

{
  "queries": [
    // base query without inline rule
    {
      "q": "fashion",
      "filter": "color = blue AND category = shirt"
    },
    // same query with inline rule
    {
      "q": "fashion",
      "filter": "color = blue AND category = shirt",
      "inlineRule": {
        "uid": "special-sales",
        "conditions": {
          "query": {
            "words": "pants",
          },
            "filter": {
              "values": {
                "color":  "yellow",
                "stock_intent": "destock"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Response:

```jsonc
{
  // [ ... ] some other fields
  "results": [
    // results without the rule
    {
      "hits": [ ... ] // documents returned without the rule
    },
    // results with the rule
    {
      // [...] some other fields
      "hits": [...], // documents returned with the rule. Here the same, because the conditions of the rules are not satisfied
      "metadata": {
        // [...] some other fields
        "inlineRuleConditionOutcomes":  {
          "satisfiesActiveCondition": true,
          "satisfiesQueryWordsCondition": {
            "missingWord": "pants"
          },
        "satifiesFilterCondition": {
          "missingConstraintOnField": {
            "field":  "stock_intent"
          },
          "unmetConstraintOnField": 
            "field": "color"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

</details>

## Generative AI tools

- [x] This PR does not use generative AI tooling
- [ ] This PR uses generative AI tooling and respect the [related policies](https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#use-of-generative-ai-tools)
    - *list of used tools and what they were used for*

### Describe the bug

The `POST` and `GET` handlers of `/indexes/{index_uid}/similar` run a vector search through `tokio::task::spawn_blocking` without first acquiring a `SearchQueue` permit. Every other search-class route acquires one: `/indexes/{index_uid}/search`, `/multi-search`, `/indexes/{index_uid}/facet-search`, `/indexes/{index_uid}/documents/fetch`, and the internal search of the chat completions route. The `search_queue` module documentation states that a search request must take a permit immediately before processing, so `/similar` is the only route that violates that contract.

Because `/similar` never enters the queue, the configured search parallelism does not apply to it. A client with the `search` action can keep an unbounded number of `/similar` requests in flight; the requests that exceed the blocking-pool capacity queue up instead of being refused. Each request runs a vector ANN search inside a blocking thread, and authentication for every protected route plus the execution of admitted searches use the same per-worker blocking pool. Once that pool is full, every request handled by the worker queues behind the similar searches, so unrelated routes stop making progress while `GET /health` keeps returning `200 available`. In addition, the similar path does not clamp `limit` by `maxTotalHits` and does not apply the index search cutoff, so a single request can also be made arbitrarily expensive by the client.

### To Reproduce

The reproduction is a deterministic in-process test. It creates an index with a `userProvided` embedder and three vectors, then builds a second app on the same `IndexScheduler` whose `SearchQueue` is saturated: the capacity is 0 and its only permit is already held. Under those conditions `/search` and `/facet-search` are refused with `503 Service Unavailable` (`Code::TooManySearchRequests`), while `/similar` returns `200 OK` and returns real hits.

Copy the file to `crates/meilisearch/tests/similar_route_bypasses_search_queue_admission.rs` and run:

```bash
cargo test -p meilisearch --test similar_route_bypasses_search_queue_admission -- --nocapture
```

<details>
<summary>Test file</summary>

```rust
// Copy to crates/meilisearch/tests/similar_route_bypasses_search_queue_admission.rs and run:
//   cargo test -p meilisearch --test similar_route_bypasses_search_queue_admission -- --nocapture

#[path = "common/mod.rs"]
mod common;

use std::num::NonZeroUsize;

use actix_web::http::StatusCode;
use actix_web::test;
use actix_web::test::TestRequest;
use actix_web::web::Data;
use common::server::Server;
use common::Value;
use meilisearch::analytics::Analytics;
use meilisearch::personalization::PersonalizationService;
use meilisearch::search_queue::SearchQueue;
use meilisearch::{create_app, ServicesData, SubscriberForSecondLayer};
use meilisearch_types::milli::progress::Progress;
use tracing::level_filters::LevelFilter;
use tracing_subscriber::Layer;

fn json_request(url: &str, body: serde_json::Value) -> actix_http::Request {
    TestRequest::post()
        .uri(url)
        .insert_header(("Content-Type", "application/json"))
        .set_payload(serde_json::to_vec(&body).unwrap())
        .to_request()
}

#[actix_rt::test]
async fn similar_route_must_take_a_search_permit() {
    let server = Server::new().await;
    let index = server.unique_index();

    // Index with a userProvided embedder and three vectors.
    let (task, code) = index
        .update_settings(Value(serde_json::json!({
            "embedders": { "manual": { "source": "userProvided", "dimensions": 3 } }
        })))
        .await;
    assert_eq!(code, StatusCode::ACCEPTED, "{task}");
    server.wait_task(task.uid()).await.succeeded();

    let (task, code) = index
        .add_documents(
            Value(serde_json::json!([
                { "id": 0, "title": "a", "_vectors": { "manual": [1.0, 0.0, 0.0] } },
                { "id": 1, "title": "b", "_vectors": { "manual": [0.9, 0.1, 0.0] } },
                { "id": 2, "title": "c", "_vectors": { "manual": [0.0, 1.0, 0.0] } },
            ])),
            None,
        )
        .await;
    assert_eq!(code, StatusCode::ACCEPTED, "{task}");
    server.wait_task(task.uid()).await.succeeded();

    // A second app on the same IndexScheduler, with a saturated search queue:
    // capacity 0 and the only permit already held.
    let queue = SearchQueue::new(0, NonZeroUsize::new(1).unwrap());
    let _held_permit = queue
        .try_get_search_permit(&Progress::quiet())
        .await
        .expect("the queue must hand out its single permit");

    let (_route_layer, route_layer_handle) = tracing_subscriber::reload::Layer::new(
        None.with_filter(
            tracing_subscriber::filter::Targets::new().with_target("", LevelFilter::OFF),
        ),
    );
    let (_stderr_layer, stderr_layer_handle) = tracing_subscriber::reload::Layer::new(
        (Box::new(
            tracing_subscriber::fmt::layer()
                .with_span_events(tracing_subscriber::fmt::format::FmtSpan::CLOSE),
        ) as Box<dyn tracing_subscriber::Layer<SubscriberForSecondLayer> + Send + Sync>)
            .with_filter(tracing_subscriber::filter::Targets::new()),
    );

    let app = test::init_service(create_app(
        ServicesData {
            index_scheduler: server.service.index_scheduler.clone().into(),
            auth: server.service.auth.clone().into(),
            search_queue: Data::new(queue),
            personalization_service: Data::new(PersonalizationService::disabled()),
            logs_route_handle: Data::new(route_layer_handle),
            logs_stderr_handle: Data::new(stderr_layer_handle),
            analytics: Data::new(Analytics::no_analytics()),
        },
        server.service.options.clone(),
        true,
    ))
    .await;

    // Control: a search on the same saturated queue is refused.
    let resp = test::call_service(
        &app,
        json_request(&format!("/indexes/{}/search", index.uid), serde_json::json!({ "q": "" })),
    )
    .await;
    let search_status = resp.status();
    assert_eq!(
        search_status,
        StatusCode::SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE,
        "the saturated SearchQueue must refuse a normal search, got {search_status}"
    );

    // Control: facet search is refused too.
    let resp = test::call_service(
        &app,
        json_request(
            &format!("/indexes/{}/facet-search", index.uid),
            serde_json::json!({ "facetName": "title", "facetQuery": "a" }),
        ),
    )
    .await;
    let facet_search_status = resp.status();
    assert_eq!(
        facet_search_status,
        StatusCode::SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE,
        "the saturated SearchQueue must refuse a facet search, got {facet_search_status}"
    );

    // The similar route must be refused as well.
    let resp = test::call_service(
        &app,
        json_request(
            &format!("/indexes/{}/similar", index.uid),
            serde_json::json!({ "id": 0, "embedder": "manual" }),
        ),
    )
    .await;
    let similar_status = resp.status();
    let similar_body: serde_json::Value =
        serde_json::from_slice(&test::read_body(resp).await).unwrap_or_default();

    assert_eq!(
        similar_status,
        StatusCode::SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE,
        "VULNERABILITY REPRODUCED: `POST /indexes/{{index_uid}}/similar` answered \
         {similar_status} while the SearchQueue was saturated (capacity 0, single permit \
         already held). The control searches were refused with {search_status} and \
         {facet_search_status}, but the similar handler never calls \
         `search_queue.try_get_search_permit` \
         (crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/similar.rs:204-247), so its vector search runs \
         in `spawn_blocking` with no admission control at all. Body: {similar_body}"
    );
}
```

</details>

<details>
<summary>Observed output on v1.54.2, abridged (panic location and thread id omitted)</summary>

```text
running 1 test

thread 'similar_route_must_take_a_search_permit' panicked:
assertion `left == right` failed: VULNERABILITY REPRODUCED: `POST /indexes/{index_uid}/similar` answered 200 OK while the SearchQueue was saturated (capacity 0, single permit already held). The control searches were refused with 503 Service Unavailable and 503 Service Unavailable, but the similar handler never calls `search_queue.try_get_search_permit` (crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/similar.rs:204-247), so its vector search runs in `spawn_blocking` with no admission control at all. Body: {"hits":[{"id":1,"title":"b"},{"id":2,"title":"c"}],"id":"0","processingTimeMs":4,"limit":20,"offset":0,"estimatedTotalHits":2}
  left: 200
 right: 503
test similar_route_must_take_a_search_permit ... FAILED

failures:

failures:
    similar_route_must_take_a_search_permit

test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 1.70s
```

</details>

The `200` body is the important part: the vector search ran while every admitted search route on the same app was being refused, so the difference is not an error path but the missing admission step. On a fixed tree, where `/similar` takes a permit, the request returns the same `503` as the controls and the test passes.

As a black-box check, run an instance with `--experimental-search-queue-size 0 --experimental-nb-searches-per-core 1` and send concurrent `/search` requests until one is refused with `503 Service Unavailable`; a `/similar` request issued in the same window still returns `200 OK`, because it never consults the queue.

### Expected behavior

`/similar` should acquire a `SearchQueue` permit before running its search, like the other search-class routes, so that the search parallelism, the queue capacity, and the drop-after limit apply to it. Its `limit` and `offset` should be clamped by `maxTotalHits`, and the vector search should honor the index search cutoff, so that a single request cannot be made arbitrarily expensive.

### Screenshots

Not applicable. The reproduction is a deterministic test.

### Meilisearch version

- Meilisearch v1.54.2, commit `a85bd66e9d7ce30e8e33a17db54b3a2de612e086`.
- actix-web 4.14.1, actix-http 3.13.3, and tokio 1.53.1, as pinned in `Cargo.lock`.
- Observed on Linux x86_64.

### Additional context

#### Root cause

**Step 1. Both handlers reach the same search implementation without a search queue.** The route is registered unconditionally next to the other index routes, and the `POST` and `GET` handlers take the index scheduler, the path, the parameters, the request, and analytics. Neither handler takes `Data<SearchQueue>`, and the shared `similar()` function they call does not take one either.

```rust
// crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/mod.rs, lines 44–48
        "" => [get(list_indexes), post(create_index)],
        "/{index_uid}" => [get(get_index), patch(update_index), delete(delete_index)],
        "/{index_uid}/documents" => sub(documents::DocumentsApi),
        "/{index_uid}/facet-search" => sub(facet_search::FacetSearchApi),
        "/{index_uid}/similar" => sub(similar::SimilarApi),
```

```rust
// crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/similar.rs, lines 96–101
pub async fn similar_get(
    index_scheduler: GuardedData<ActionPolicy<{ actions::SEARCH }>, Data<IndexScheduler>>,
    index_uid: web::Path<String>,
    params: AwebQueryParameter<SimilarQueryGet, DeserrQueryParamError>,
    req: HttpRequest,
    analytics: web::Data<Analytics>,
) -> Result<HttpResponse, ResponseError> {
```

```rust
// crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/similar.rs, lines 177–183
pub async fn similar_post(
    index_scheduler: GuardedData<ActionPolicy<{ actions::SEARCH }>, Data<IndexScheduler>>,
    index_uid: web::Path<String>,
    params: AwebJson<SimilarQuery, DeserrJsonError>,
    req: HttpRequest,
    analytics: web::Data<Analytics>,
) -> Result<HttpResponse, ResponseError> {
```

**Step 2. The search queue is the only admission control for search-class requests.** Its module documentation states that every search request should take a permit right before processing. The production instance builds it with the configured queue size and a parallelism of the available parallelism multiplied by the configured searches per core, and all of the search routes listed above call `try_get_search_permit`.

```rust
// crates/meilisearch/src/search_queue.rs, lines 1–11
//! This file implements a queue of searches to process and the ability to control how many searches can be run in parallel.
//! We need this because we don't want to process more search requests than the available CPU cores.
//! That slows down everything and consumes RAM for no reason.
//! The steps to do a search are to get the `SearchQueue` data structure and try to get a search permit.
//! This can fail if the queue is full, and we need to drop your search request to register a new one.
//!
//! ### How to do a search request
//!
//! In order to do a search request you should try to get a search permit.
//! Retrieve the `SearchQueue` structure from actix-web (`search_queue: Data<SearchQueue>`)
//! and right before processing the search, call the `SearchQueue::try_get_search_permit` method: `search_queue.try_get_search_permit().await?;`
```

```rust
// crates/meilisearch/src/main.rs, lines 167–175
    let search_queue = SearchQueue::new(
        opt.experimental_search_queue_size,
        available_parallelism()
            .unwrap_or(NonZeroUsize::new(2).unwrap())
            .checked_mul(opt.experimental_nb_searches_per_core)
            .unwrap_or(NonZeroUsize::MAX),
    )
    .with_time_to_abort(Duration::from_secs(
        usize::from(opt.experimental_drop_search_after) as u64
    ));
```

For comparison, `/facet-search`, which is also an index-scoped search route, takes the queue and the permit before it touches the index.

```rust
// crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/facet_search.rs, lines 302–316
pub async fn search(
    index_scheduler: GuardedData<ActionPolicy<{ actions::SEARCH }>, Data<IndexScheduler>>,
    search_queue: Data<SearchQueue>,
    index_uid: web::Path<String>,
    params: AwebJson<FacetSearchQuery, DeserrJsonError>,
    req: HttpRequest,
    analytics: web::Data<Analytics>,
) -> Result<HttpResponse, ResponseError> {
    // Progress is not used, we use the quiet progress to avoid logging any steps.
    let progress = Progress::quiet();
    let index_uid = IndexUid::try_from(index_uid.into_inner())?;

    let before_search = time::OffsetDateTime::now_utc();

    let permit = search_queue.try_get_search_permit(&progress).await?;
```

The remaining permit call sites are `crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/search.rs` (four sites, for the new and legacy search pipelines), `crates/meilisearch/src/routes/multi_search.rs` (two sites), `crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/documents.rs` (two sites, used when the document fetch queue is enabled), and `crates/meilisearch/src/routes/chats/chat_completions.rs` (one site). `crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/similar.rs` has none. The route was added two weeks after the queue was wired into the search, multi-search, and facet-search routes (`git log -S try_get_search_permit`), so this looks like an omission rather than an intentional exemption.

**Step 3. The shared `similar()` function runs the search directly in `spawn_blocking`.** It has no permit to acquire and no queue argument to pass, so the search parallelism is never consulted on this path.

```rust
// crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/similar.rs, lines 204–233
async fn similar(
    index_scheduler: GuardedData<ActionPolicy<{ actions::SEARCH }>, Data<IndexScheduler>>,
    index_uid: IndexUid,
    mut query: SimilarQuery,
) -> Result<SimilarResult, ResponseError> {
    // Progress is not used, we use the quiet progress to avoid logging any steps.
    let progress = Progress::quiet();

    let (index_scheduler, auth_filter) = index_scheduler.into_inner();
    let search_rules = auth_filter.get_index_search_rules(&index_uid);

    // Tenant token search_rules.
    if let Some(search_rules) = search_rules.clone() {
        add_search_rules(&mut query.filter, search_rules);
    }

    let progress_clone = progress.clone();
    let result = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || {
        let _step = progress_clone.update_progress_scoped(TotalProcessingTimeStep::Process);

        perform_similar(
            &index_scheduler,
            index_uid,
            query,
            &progress_clone,
            &auth_filter,
            search_rules,
        )
    })
    .await;
```

**Step 4. The missing permit removes the concurrency bound, and the request cost is user-controlled as well.** Without the queue, nothing limits how many similar searches can be in flight. Each request forwards the user-provided `limit` to the vector store as the number of neighbors to retrieve, and the result vectors are pre-allocated from the same value. `limit` is a plain `usize`, and the similar path does not clamp it by `maxTotalHits`; `maxTotalHits` is read after the search and only feeds `estimatedTotalHits`. The similar path also does not apply the index search cutoff: the `nns_by_item` call in `Similar::execute` has no deadline parameter, unlike `nns_by_vector`, and `perform_similar` never reads the deadline.

```rust
// crates/meilisearch/src/search/mod.rs, lines 1104–1106
