### Describe the bug

Meilisearch reads HTTP request bodies without any deadline. A client that sends a complete request head plus the first bytes of the body and then keeps the connection open without sending anything else leaves the handler future pending forever. Two production paths are affected: `POST /mcp`, which reads the body before the experimental-feature check and before any authentication, and `POST` and `PUT /indexes/{index_uid}/documents`, which stream the body into a temporary file.

Each stalled request holds a task, a socket file descriptor, and the bytes already sent: the document routes write them to a temporary file under `--http-payload-size-limit` (default 100 MB), and `POST /mcp` buffers them for JSON parsing. Nothing releases these resources except the peer closing the connection or the process exiting. Because a single client can open many connections, the instance can exhaust its file descriptors, disk space, or memory and stop serving all clients.

