### Describe the bug

When `xtask bench` downloads a remote asset whose workload entry omits `sha256`, it writes the response directly to the asset's canonical cache pathname. If Ctrl-C cancels the workload after a response prefix has been written but before the body reaches EOF, the download future is dropped and the incomplete canonical file remains.

A later invocation opens that file during the cache lookup. Because `check_sha256` returns `true` when no expected digest is configured, `fetch_assets` treats the incomplete file as a valid cache hit, skips the remote request, and returns success.

```text
missing hashless remote asset
-> open/truncate canonical cache pathname
-> write a response prefix
-> Ctrl-C aborts the workload owner
-> dropping fetch_assets drops its JoinSet and aborts the download child
-> incomplete canonical file remains
-> next run checks the same path with sha256 = None
-> check_sha256 returns true
-> fetch_assets reuses the incomplete file without another request
```

This violates the cache-publication invariant: a canonical asset should become reusable only after the response reaches EOF, buffered writes complete successfully, and all configured validation succeeds. No cancellation point should leave an uncommitted artifact at a pathname that a later invocation interprets as committed.

The source-level chain below is pinned to commit `fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018`.

1. The benchmark workload runs in a spawned owner task, and its `AbortHandle` is passed to the Ctrl-C handler:
   https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/xtask/src/bench/mod.rs#L114-L169

```rust
