### Expected behavior

Once `CreateMultipartUpload` has succeeded, cancellation should retain ownership of the multipart transaction until both local child work and remote state reach a defined terminal outcome:

```text
observe cancellation
-> prevent new part requests and retries
-> abort and drain every in-flight child task
-> send AbortMultipartUpload for the upload ID
-> await cleanup success or preserve an explicit cleanup failure
-> return Error::AbortedTask
```

After `multipart_stream_to_s3` returns `Error::AbortedTask`:

- no child task started by that invocation should issue another request;
- no retry should begin after cancellation has been observed;
- the created multipart upload should have an abort request issued unless it was already completed;
- cleanup failure should remain observable rather than being hidden by the cancellation result.

Possible repair shapes include:

- own part tasks in a cancellation-aware collection and abort/drain them on every early return;
- use a guard that owns the upload ID and performs multipart cleanup until explicitly committed;
- propagate cancellation into the retry operation so it cannot schedule a later request;
- perform `CompleteMultipartUpload` as the commit step and disarm cleanup only after it succeeds; and
- preserve cleanup errors or enqueue bounded cleanup work when synchronous abort does not succeed.

