### Describe the bug

`SearchQueue::try_get_search_permit` transfers a `oneshot::Sender<Permit>` to the search scheduler and then awaits the corresponding receiver. If the request future is cancelled after the scheduler has accepted that sender but before a permit is delivered, cancellation drops the receiver while the scheduler continues to retain the sender in its bounded `Vec` queue.

The scheduler does not prune closed oneshot senders before using `queue.len()` for capacity enforcement, random eviction, waiter selection, or the `searches_waiting` metric. A request that no longer exists can therefore continue to consume a queue slot and participate in admission decisions.

The vulnerable transition is:

```text
request creates (Sender<Permit>, Receiver<Permit>)
    -> Sender is transferred to SearchQueue::run
    -> scheduler stores Sender in queue
    -> request is cancelled while awaiting Receiver<Permit>
    -> Receiver is dropped
    -> scheduler retains the now-closed Sender
    -> stale entry remains counted in queue.len()
```

This can produce user-visible effects when the queue is under load:

- a live request can be randomly evicted and receive `TooManySearchRequests` while a cancelled waiter remains in the queue;
- a released permit can first be delivered to a closed receiver, adding an unnecessary scheduling turn before live work proceeds;
- `searches_waiting` can report cancelled requests as live pending work.

For example, with `parallelism = 1` and `capacity = 2`, request A can hold the running permit, cancelled request B can remain as a stale waiter, and live request C can occupy the second apparent slot. When request D arrives, the scheduler sees a full queue and randomly removes either B or C. If it removes C, a live request is rejected while the cancelled B remains admitted.

All source links below are pinned to commit `fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018`.

1. `try_get_search_permit` sends the waiter ticket to the scheduler before awaiting the receiver:
   [`crates/meilisearch/src/search_queue.rs#L177-L183`](https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/meilisearch/src/search_queue.rs#L177-L183)

```rust
let (sender, receiver) = oneshot::channel();
self.sender.send(sender).await.map_err(|_| MeilisearchHttpError::SearchLimiterIsDown)?;
let permit = receiver
    .await
    .map_err(|_| MeilisearchHttpError::TooManySearchRequests(self.capacity))?;
```

Once `self.sender.send(sender).await` completes, the scheduler independently owns the sender. Cancelling the caller at or before `receiver.await` drops only the receiver; there is no cancellation guard that removes the transferred sender.

2. The scheduler retains these senders in a plain `Vec` and uses its raw length for capacity and metrics:
   [`crates/meilisearch/src/search_queue.rs#L121-L171`](https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/meilisearch/src/search_queue.rs#L121-L171)

```rust
let mut queue: Vec<oneshot::Sender<Permit>> = Default::default();

// ...

} else if queue.len() >= capacity {
    let remove = rng.gen_range(0..queue.len());
    let thing = queue.swap_remove(remove);
    drop(thing);
}
queue.push(search_request);

// ...

metric_searches_waiting.store(queue.len(), Ordering::Relaxed);
```

There is no `Sender::is_closed()` check or other cancellation-aware removal before `queue.len()` controls admission.

3. Permit delivery also selects directly from the same population without skipping closed receivers in the same scheduler turn:
   [`crates/meilisearch/src/search_queue.rs#L131-L138`](https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/meilisearch/src/search_queue.rs#L131-L138)

```rust
_ = search_finished.recv() => {
    searches_running = searches_running.saturating_sub(1);
    if !queue.is_empty() {
        let remove = rng.gen_range(0..queue.len());
        let channel = queue.swap_remove(remove);
        let _ = channel.send(Permit { sender: sender.clone() });
    }
},
```

If the selected receiver was cancelled, `send` fails. The stale entry has already affected queue length, eviction, selection, and metrics before that failure reveals it.

4. This wait occurs directly in HTTP request paths, including normal search, multi-search, and facet search:

- [`crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/search.rs#L543-L559`](https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/search.rs#L543-L559)
- [`crates/meilisearch/src/routes/multi_search.rs#L157-L196`](https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/meilisearch/src/routes/multi_search.rs#L157-L196)
- [`crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/facet_search.rs#L292-L336`](https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/meilisearch/src/routes/indexes/facet_search.rs#L292-L336)

A disconnected client, upstream timeout, or cancelled request handler can therefore reach this window while waiting for search admission.

### To Reproduce

The following focused whitebox test intentionally asserts the current bad state. Therefore, the test passing means the stale-waiter state is reachable. It does not use sleeps or depend on the scheduler's random eviction choice.

Steps:

1. Append the test-only module below to `crates/meilisearch/src/search_queue.rs`.
2. Run the targeted unit-test command.
3. Observe that the task waiting for a permit is successfully cancelled, but `searches_waiting()` remains `1` afterward.

<details>
<summary>Full whitebox test code</summary>

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod cancellation_tests {
    use super::*;

    #[tokio::test(flavor = "current_thread")]
    async fn cancelled_permit_waiter_remains_counted_in_search_queue() {
        let queue = Arc::new(SearchQueue::new(1, NonZeroUsize::new(1).unwrap()));

        // Saturate the only running-search slot so the next request must wait.
        let active_permit = queue
            .try_get_search_permit()
            .await
            .expect("the first request should receive a permit");
        assert_eq!(queue.searches_waiting(), 0);

        let waiting_queue = Arc::clone(&queue);
        let waiting_task = tokio::spawn(async move {
            waiting_queue.try_get_search_permit().await
        });

        // This is the acceptance point: the scheduler has stored the sender in
        // its private queue and published the corresponding queue length.
        tokio::time::timeout(Duration::from_secs(1), async {
            loop {
                if queue.searches_waiting() == 1 {
                    break;
                }
                tokio::task::yield_now().await;
            }
        })
        .await
        .expect("the waiter should be accepted by the scheduler");
        assert!(
            !waiting_task.is_finished(),
            "the accepted waiter should still be awaiting its permit"
        );

        // The request future is cancelled while it awaits the permit. This
        // drops its receiver, but the scheduler already owns the sender.
        waiting_task.abort();
        let cancellation = waiting_task
            .await
            .expect_err("aborting the waiter should cancel its task");
        assert!(cancellation.is_cancelled());

        // Bug-existence assertion: dropping the receiver does not remove the
        // sender from the scheduler queue, so the cancelled waiter remains a
        // counted capacity slot after cancellation.
        assert_eq!(queue.searches_waiting(), 1);

        drop(active_permit);
    }
}
```

</details>

